                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\,$where $K$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139534565 true] [17592186295778 1113 #uuid "94f3bb43-15c5-5035-aaa0-448fb1039607" 13194139534565 true] [17592186295778 1145 "To find the general solution, we separate variables and integrate both sides with respect to $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.03(T-25) \\\\ \\frac{1}{T-25}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.03 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{T-25}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int -0.03\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}T}{T-25} & =-0.03\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-0.03t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a perfectly valid general solution. However, if we want to write $T=T(t)$ explicitly, then we can solve for $T.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-0.03t+C \\\\ |T-25| & ={e}^{-0.03t+C} \\\\ |T-25| & =K\\,{e}^{-0.03t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary positive constant.\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be both positive and negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =25+K\\,{e}^{-0.03t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534565 true] [17592186295779 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295780 13194139534565 true] [17592186295779 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534565 true] [17592186295779 1041 "field-1" 13194139534565 true] [17592186295779 1104 #edb/ref 17592186295780 13194139534565 true] [17592186295779 1110 #uuid "225b5a66-5dc6-5fd3-84e1-88a5c5dfd035" 13194139534565 true] [17592186295780 1034 "e^{-0.03t}" 13194139534565 true] [17592186295780 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534565 true] [17592186295780 1111 #uuid "a9226fd8-50a0-5a85-b